  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">U4 Anti-corruption Resource Centre 2022-2026</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The grant agreement covers support to the U4 Anti-corruption Resource Centre for the period 2022-2026. Climate change is one of the thematic areas U4 focusses on in the agreement.

The Project&apos;s expected effects on society (Impact) are contained in the following Strategic Goals, as defined in U4&apos;s Strategy 2022-2026.
1) Greater impact on the ground.
2) A coherent, global approach to anti-corruption.
3) Addressing the complexities of corruption head-on.
4) Pushing anti-corruption up the agenda.

For each strategic goal, an objective has been defined (in U4&apos;s Action Plan 2022-2026) as follows:

Strategic goal 1 (greater impact on the ground):
Objective 1: U4 will shift even further towards supporting local practice, strengthening our partners&apos; work, and achieving greater impact &apos;on the ground&apos;.

Strategic goal 2 (develop a coherent, global approach to anti-corruption):
Objective 2: Offer strategic advice, support and evidence to strengthen the coordination and development of joint positions among our U4 partners in their anti-corruption responses and in international policymaking; and to help them identify common ground and possible collaboration with the &apos;not-so-likeminded&apos; where strategic interests coincide - such as corruption that undermines responses to climate change.

Strategic goal 3 (addressing the complexities of corruption):
Objective 3: Solving a myriad of connected corruption-related challenges across the globe and in different sectors, including in places and spaces conventionally seen as distant from the problem of large-scale corruption.

Strategic goal 4 (pushing anti-corruption higher up the agenda in our partners&apos; home countries):
Objective 4: Making the case with evidence that anti-corruption is a key driver for change in U4 partner countries, demonstrating that corruption is related to a wide range of local, regional and global challenges, and that tackling corruption requires a transitional and cross-government effort.</narrative>
  </description>
